      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Penile fracture is an uncommon, but well-described, entity that requires emergent treatment. The classic, \"text-book\" history, a blow to the erect penis accompanied by a \"snap,\" pain, and immediate detumescence, is not universally present. We report 2 cases of \"false\" penile fracture, a condition that closely mimics \"true\" penile fracture. Perhaps the most distinguishing symptoms are the absence of the \"snap\" and gradual detumescence, both of which suggest false fracture but are not specific. It is our intention to bring this condition to the attention of general urologists who may see it in clinical practice and to guide them in its management."},
